{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P18",
    "title": "Button interrupt does nothing - NVIC not enabled vs no handler installed",
    "layer": "3 Drivers",
    "description": "The latency program from P04 with one button press, in two broken variants. \"not_enabled\": main writes the priority but never sets the IRQ's bit in NVIC_ISER. The GPIO block still latches the edge and the NVIC marks IRQ 21 pending, but a disabled interrupt is never taken: the program runs on as if nothing happened. \"no_handler\": the interrupt is enabled, but the vector table entry for IRQ 21 still points at Default_Handler, the catch-all loop: the core takes the interrupt and then spins forever.\n",
    "program": "latency",
    "options": {
      "critical_section_len": 60
    },
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 12000
    },
    "stimuli": [
      {
        "action": "button_press",
        "at_us": 30
      }
    ],
    "variants": [
      {
        "name": "not_enabled",
        "hidden_symptom": "Pressing the button does nothing at all; the main loop keeps running as if no edge happened.",
        "faults": [
          {
            "type": "irq_not_enabled"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "name": "no_handler",
        "hidden_symptom": "The program runs until the first button press, then freezes; no fault is reported.",
        "faults": [
          {
            "type": "missing_irq_handler"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "expected": {
      "summary": "not_enabled shows the IRQ pending but never taken (no exception entry, presses=0, the run ends on max_cycles); no_handler enters IRQ_GPIO at Default_Handler and stays there.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "no_faults",
          "variant": "not_enabled"
        },
        {
          "check": "min_events",
          "variant": "not_enabled",
          "kind": "irq_pend",
          "to": "IRQ_GPIO",
          "count": 1
        },
        {
          "check": "no_event",
          "variant": "not_enabled",
          "kind": "exception_entry"
        },
        {
          "check": "global_equals",
          "variant": "not_enabled",
          "name": "presses",
          "value": 0
        },
        {
          "check": "stop_reason_contains",
          "variant": "not_enabled",
          "text": "max_cycles"
        },
        {
          "check": "min_events",
          "variant": "no_handler",
          "kind": "exception_entry",
          "to": "ISR:IRQ_GPIO",
          "count": 1
        },
        {
          "check": "stop_reason_contains",
          "variant": "no_handler",
          "text": "Default_Handler"
        },
        {
          "check": "global_equals",
          "variant": "no_handler",
          "name": "presses",
          "value": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "not_enabled: an irq_pend event \"GPIO edge on pin 14\" and then nothing: no exception_entry, no marker pin, presses stays 0, and the run only ends at max_cycles. On the board NVIC_ISPR0 bit 21 is 1 (pending) while NVIC_ISER0 bit 21 is 0 (disabled). no_handler: exception_entry IRQ_GPIO with handler=Default_Handler's address (compare with image_map.vectors and the symbol in \"r2s disasm latency\"), then a kernel event \"Default_Handler reached for IRQ_GPIO (IPSR=37)\" and the run stops there. IPSR = 16 + 21 = 37 is the number to read in the debugger.\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "NVIC_EnableIRQ (or the NVIC_ISER write) missing, or written with the wrong bit/word: IRQ n is bit n % 32 of NVIC_ISER[n / 32].",
      "The peripheral's own interrupt enable (here the GPIO edge enable) set but not the NVIC's, or the reverse: both are needed.",
      "Handler name misspelled (GPIO_IRQHandler vs IO_IRQ_BANK0_IRQHandler), so the weak alias to Default_Handler stays in the vector table.",
      "Handler defined in C++ without extern \"C\", so its mangled name does not override the weak symbol."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "In GDB read NVIC_ISER0 (0xE000E100) and NVIC_ISPR0 (0xE000E200): pending but not enabled is the first variant.",
      "When the board 'hangs', halt it and 'p/x $xpsr': the low 9 bits (IPSR) give the exception number; 37 = IRQ 21. 'info symbol $pc' shows Default_Handler.",
      "Check the vector table entry: 'x/xw 0x10000000 + 4*37' must be your handler's address with bit 0 set.",
      "'arm-none-eabi-nm' the ELF: if the handler symbol is missing or is a weak alias, the name does not match the startup file."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "An interrupt needs three things to run your code: the peripheral must raise it, the NVIC must have it enabled, and the vector table must point at your handler. The first variant fails the second condition silently; the second variant fails the third and parks the core in a loop. Reading the pending/enable bits and IPSR tells the two apart in a minute.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "Pressing the button does nothing. In one build the program keeps running normally; in another build it freezes on the first press."
  },
